{"parentUuid":"1ce2984c-985b-408e-9b88-be5283035326","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"gen_01KTPZR2THB5A0CP7DZ5KTYYQN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Every request to the app is now logged to the console.\n\nI created `proxy.ts` at the project root — in Next.js 16, `proxy.ts` replaces the deprecated `middleware.ts` as the file that intercepts every incoming request:\n\n```ts\nimport { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from 'next/server'\n\nexport function proxy(request: NextRequest) {\n  console.log(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${request.method} ${request.nextUrl.pathname}${request.nextUrl.search}`)\n  return NextResponse.next()\n}\n```\n\nI verified it by starting the dev server and sending two requests — both showed up in the server console:\n\n```\n[2026-06-09T20:03:43.301Z] GET /\n[2026-06-09T20:03:45.504Z] GET /some-page?q=1\n```\n\nIt logs the timestamp, HTTP method, path, and query string for all routes (pages, API routes, even 404s), since there's no `matcher` config restricting it. If you want to exclude static assets like `/_next/*` from the logs, you can add a `config.matcher` export to the file.\n\nOne side note: starting the dev server caused Next.js to auto-update `tsconfig.json` and generate `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` (a new Next 16 behavior you can disable with `agentRules: false` in `next.config.ts`). Those changes are in the working tree alongside `proxy.ts`."}],"model":"claude-fable-5","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":311,"cache_read_input_tokens":34884,"output_tokens":477,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":311},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"4dde9c97-872c-4d1a-9a6e-b45f51a678a5","timestamp":"2026-06-09T20:04:05.447Z","userType":"ex